{"abstract":"Importance: Epilepsy is a common neurological disorder in childhood. It remains unclear whether early-onset sepsis is associated with epilepsy and to what extent early-onset meningitis may contribute to its development. Objective: To study the association between early-onset neonatal infection and childhood epilepsy. Design, Setting, and Participants: Nationwide, population-based, register-based cohort study of all Danish live-born singletons with at least 35 completed gestational weeks at birth without major congenital anomalies in Denmark between 1997 and 2013 with follow-up until 2021 or age 18 years, whichever came first. Data were analyzed from February 2024 to January 2025. Exposures: Early-onset infection was defined as an invasive bacterial infection during the first week of life. Diagnosed infection was defined by diagnostic codes of sepsis or meningitis, while culture-positive infection only included those with bacterial pathogens cultured from blood or cerebrospinal fluid. Main outcomes and measures: Epilepsy was defined as a hospital diagnosis or at least 2 redeemed prescriptions of antiepileptic medication. The association between diagnosed sepsis and epilepsy was investigated with multivariable Cox regression to estimate adjusted hazard ratios (HR) with 95% CIs. Associations for the remaining definitions of infection were investigated using person-time incidence rate ratios (IRR). Results: Among 981 869 children, the median (IQR) gestational age was 40 (39-41) weeks, 501 615 (51%) were male, 8154 (0.8%) were diagnosed with sepsis, and 152 (<0.1%) were diagnosed with meningitis. Of these, 257 children had culture-positive sepsis and 32 had culture-positive meningitis. The incidence rate of epilepsy was 1.6 per 1000 person-years for children with diagnosed sepsis compared with 0.9 per 1000 person-years for children without an infection, resulting in an adjusted HR of 1.85 (95% CI, 1.60-2.13). Culture-positive sepsis was also associated with an increased risk of epilepsy (IRR, 2.70; 95% CI, 1.08-5.56), while the highest risks were observed in children with meningitis both diagnosed (IRR, 9.85; 95% CI, 5.52-16.27) and verified by culture (IRR, 16.04; 95% CI, 5.21-37.46). Conclusions and relevance: In this nationwide cohort study, early-onset neonatal sepsis was associated with an approximately 2-fold increased risk of childhood epilepsy. As expected, early-onset meningitis was associated with an even higher risk of epilepsy with an approximately 10-fold increase.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Andersen, Mads","Matthiesen, Niels Bjerregård","Murra, May","Nielsen, Stine Yde","Henriksen, Tine Brink"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-822782"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Andersen, Mads"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"early onset neonatal infection and epilepsy in children","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.19090","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-822782","identifier":[{"id":"40622714","type":"pmid"},{"id":"105010664427","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC12235498","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["au-ffc7e40f-865b-4281-9f34-1b99f7356999","sdu-e677e4b6-0dd5-4816-8cca-b6bf623aa125"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4D2254235EB56BFEE05ACDB96D496181"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.19090","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/295396788/Open_Access_Version.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Paediatrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Clinical Microbiology (Vejle)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Lillebælt Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a6f87077-4675-4356-8b30-c350d79d0b42","name":"Andersen, Mads","orcid":"0000-0002-9682-9272","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels Bjerregård","key":"matthiesenpau","last":"Matthiesen","locid":"493b9487-1f73-4b4e-8865-6cf1b8080b27","name":"Matthiesen, Niels Bjerregård","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"May","key":"murrapau","last":"Murra","locid":"8af498a4-a250-44e7-b234-4284ff835d28","name":"Murra, May","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stine Yde","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f3a5fd5a-d6cf-4dc2-b10e-acdaa9be8dce","name":"Nielsen, Stine Yde","orcid":"0000-0001-5808-4495","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tine Brink","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"1fbd1982-a1cb-4687-9250-60b91463b802","name":"Henriksen, Tine Brink","orcid":"0000-0001-6933-8294","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40622714"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.19090","issn":["25743805"],"issue":"7","journal":"JAMA network open","paperid":"e2519090","publisher":"American Medical Association","status":"p","title":"JAMA network open","title_org":"JAMA Network Open","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105010664427"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ffc7e40f-865b-4281-9f34-1b99f7356999","sdu-e677e4b6-0dd5-4816-8cca-b6bf623aa125"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Early-Onset Neonatal Infection and Epilepsy in Children","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-822782","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Objectives: We aimed to study the association between early-onset neonatal infection in near-term and term children and school performance based on mandatory tests in reading and mathematics. Methods: We conducted a nationwide register-based cohort study including all Danish near-term and term singletons born from 1997 to 2009. Early-onset infection was defined as an invasive bacterial infection during the first week of life. Infections were categorized into diagnosed sepsis or meningitis, and culture-positive sepsis or meningitis verified by bacteria cultured from blood or cerebrospinal fluid. Multivariable mixed model linear regression was used to estimate mean differences in test scores, expressed as standard deviation scores (SDSs) with 95% CI. Results: Among 638 402 children, 2 362 046 test scores were available from 9 to 15 years of age. A total of 5347 children were diagnosed with sepsis and 73 with meningitis, while 135 also had culture-positive sepsis and 20 had culture-positive meningitis. Diagnosed sepsis was associated with lower test scores with mean differences in reading of –0.08 SDS (95% CI: –0.10 to –0.05) and mathematics of –0.08 SDS (95% CI: –0.10 to –0.05). Diagnosed meningitis was associated with even lower test scores with mean differences in reading of –0.22 SDS (95% CI: –0.43 to 0.00) and mathematics of –0.31 SDS (95% CI: –0.55 to –0.07). These findings remained consistent even in sibling-matched analyses. Similar results were also found when only culture-positive infections were compared with the reference population. Sepsis caused by Escherichia coli showed the largest reduction in test scores, whereas group B Streptococcus appeared not to affect point estimates. Discussion: Early-onset sepsis was associated with modest reductions in test scores. This may be insignificant for the individual but could be important on a public health level. Early-onset meningitis was associated with more substantial reductions, emphasizing the severity of this condition even in children able to attend public school.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Andersen, Mads","Matthiesen, Niels Bjerregård","Murra, May","Nielsen, Stine Yde","Henriksen, Tine Brink"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-804056"],"filters":{"added":"2025-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Andersen, Mads"],"keyword":["Academic Performance statistics numerical data","Adolescent","Child","Cognitive function","Cohort Studies","Denmark epidemiology","Early onset infection","Female","Humans","Infant Newborn","Male","Neonatal meningitis","Neonatal sepsis","Reading","Registries","School performance","Sepsis epidemiology"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"early onset neonatal infection and school performance a danish nationwide population based cohort study","src":["au","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85214848158","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-804056","identifier":[{"id":"85214848158","type":"scopus"},{"id":"39725076","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-5a294d10-138f-41e9-80ac-36ce2eab5a79","sdu-bbfbef35-58bf-4482-862f-691031c8be82"],"keyword":["Academic Performance/statistics & numerical data","Adolescent","Child","Cognitive function","Cohort Studies","Denmark/epidemiology","Early-onset infection","Female","Humans","Infant, Newborn","Male","Meningitis/epidemiology","Neonatal meningitis","Neonatal sepsis","Reading","Registries","School performance","Sepsis/epidemiology"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-871D1A27718A38560B81F7C7F20BDBC7"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Paediatrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Clinical Microbiology (Vejle)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Lillebælt Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a6f87077-4675-4356-8b30-c350d79d0b42","name":"Andersen, Mads","orcid":"0000-0002-9682-9272","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels Bjerregård","key":"matthiesenpau","last":"Matthiesen","locid":"493b9487-1f73-4b4e-8865-6cf1b8080b27","name":"Matthiesen, Niels Bjerregård","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"May","key":"murrapau","last":"Murra","locid":"8af498a4-a250-44e7-b234-4284ff835d28","name":"Murra, May","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stine Yde","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f3a5fd5a-d6cf-4dc2-b10e-acdaa9be8dce","name":"Nielsen, Stine Yde","orcid":"0000-0001-5808-4495","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tine Brink","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"1fbd1982-a1cb-4687-9250-60b91463b802","name":"Henriksen, Tine Brink","orcid":"0000-0001-6933-8294","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["39725076"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.cmi.2024.12.029","issn":["1198743X","14690691"],"issn_org":["1198743X"],"issue":"3","journal":"Clinical Microbiology and Infection","pages":"389-395","publisher":"Elsevier BV","publisher_org":"Elsevier B.V.","status":"p","title":"Clinical Microbiology and Infection","volume":"31"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85214848158"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-5a294d10-138f-41e9-80ac-36ce2eab5a79","sdu-bbfbef35-58bf-4482-862f-691031c8be82"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Early-onset neonatal infection and school performance : a Danish nationwide population-based cohort study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-804056","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Background: Early-onset neonatal infections are among the most common neonatal diseases. However, the long-term outcomes of the infections are not well understood. Objective: To study the association between early-onset neonatal infection and attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) and autism spectrum disorder (ASD). Methods: A nationwide register-based cohort study was conducted, including near-term and term children born between 1997 and 2013 with follow-up until 2021. An early-onset infection was defined as an invasive bacterial infection occurring within the first week of life, including both physician-assigned diagnoses and positive bacterial cultures. ADHD and ASD were defined by diagnoses or prescriptions of relevant medication. Associations between sepsis and the neurodevelopmental disorders were investigated using multivariable Cox regression to estimate adjusted hazard ratios (HR), whereas associations with meningitis were examined using person-time incidence rate ratios (IRR). Sibling-matched analyses were also conducted for associations with sepsis. Results: A total of 981,869 children were included, with 8154 defined as having sepsis and 152 defined as having meningitis. Among these, only 257 children had culture-positive sepsis, whereas 32 had culture-positive meningitis. The incidence rate of ADHD and ASD for children with sepsis was 4.5 per 1000 and 3.3 per 1000 person-years, respectively. Sepsis was associated with an increased adjusted likelihood of both ADHD (HR 1.28, 95% CI 1.17, 1.39) and ASD (HR 1.43, 95% CI 1.30, 1.58). However, sibling-matched analyses especially attenuated the association with ADHD (HR 1.12, 95% CI 0.93, 1.34). Point estimates suggested that children with meningitis also had an increased likelihood of both ADHD (IRR 1.77, 95% CI 0.88, 3.17) and ASD (IRR 2.05, 95% CI 0.89, 4.04). Conclusions: Early-onset sepsis was associated with an increased likelihood of ASD, whereas the majority of the association with ADHD could be explained by unmeasured shared familial confounding.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Andersen, Mads","Matthiesen, Niels Bjerregård","Murra, May","Nielsen, Stine Yde","Henriksen, Tine Brink"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-815972"],"filters":{"added":"2025-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Andersen, Mads"],"keyword":["Attention Deficit Disorder with Hyperactivity","Autism spectrum disorder","Bacterial Infections","Bacterial meningitis","Neonatal sepsis","Neurodevelopmental disorders"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-SDR"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-SDR","DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"early onset neonatal infection and attention deficit hyperactivity and autism spectrum disorder a nationwide cohort study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/ppe.70036","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-815972","identifier":[{"id":"105008452091","type":"scopus"},{"id":"40528692","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-71cafe95-bafa-4654-bafb-a7cb73165438"],"keyword":["attention deficit disorder with hyperactivity","autism spectrum disorder","bacterial infections","bacterial meningitis","neonatal sepsis","neurodevelopmental disorders"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-A0D11749DD68335D9CFBEACA068BAD4A"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/ppe.70036","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Paediatrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"region of southern denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-SDR","l3nl":"Region of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"Region of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region of Southern Denmark","namevar":["Lillebælt Hospital"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-SDR","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Mads","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"a6f87077-4675-4356-8b30-c350d79d0b42","name":"Andersen, Mads","orcid":"0000-0002-9682-9272","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Niels Bjerregård","key":"matthiesenpau","last":"Matthiesen","locid":"493b9487-1f73-4b4e-8865-6cf1b8080b27","name":"Matthiesen, Niels Bjerregård","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"May","key":"murrapau","last":"Murra","name":"Murra, May","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Stine Yde","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"f3a5fd5a-d6cf-4dc2-b10e-acdaa9be8dce","name":"Nielsen, Stine Yde","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tine Brink","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"1fbd1982-a1cb-4687-9250-60b91463b802","name":"Henriksen, Tine Brink","orcid":"0000-0001-6933-8294","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["40528692"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/ppe.70036","issn":["02695022","13653016"],"issn_org":["02695022"],"journal":"Paediatric and Perinatal Epidemiology","publisher":"John Wiley & Sons Ltd","status":"e","title":"Paediatric and Perinatal Epidemiology"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-105008452091"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region of Southern Denmark","Universities"],"PST":["E-Published (ahead of print)"]},"sid":["au-71cafe95-bafa-4654-bafb-a7cb73165438"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Early-Onset Neonatal Infection and Attention Deficit Hyperactivity and Autism Spectrum Disorder: : A Nationwide Cohort Study","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-815972","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The partial nitritation/anammox process is a popular process for sidestream nitrogen removal, but the process is sensitive to disturbances and requires extensive surveillance and monitoring for optimal performance. We followed two parallel sidestream full-scale deammonification reactors treating digester centrate for a year with high time-resolution of both online sensor data and microbial community as measured by Nanopore DNA sequencing. DNA surveillance revealed system disturbances and allowed for detection of process and equipment upsets, and it facilitated remediating operational actions. Surveillance of anammox bacteria (Ca. Brocadia) revealed unexpected variations, and the composition and dynamics of the flanking community indicated causes for occasional process disturbances with poor nitrogen removal. Monitoring the ammonia oxidizing bacteria (AOB) and nitrite oxidizing bacteria (NOB) could potentially allow reactor operation with increased dissolved oxygen (DO), yielding higher ammonia conversion while keeping NOB in control. The use of fast and frequent DNA sequencing (sampling 3–5 times a week, analysed once per week) was an important supplement, and in many cases superior, to the online sensor data for process surveillance, understanding and control.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Andersen, Martin Hjorth","Thomsen, Lisette","Stokholm-Bjerregaard, Mikkel","Eriksen, Søren","Hove Hansen, Susan","Albertsen, Mads","Nielsen, Per Halkjær"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-726487"],"filters":{"added":"2024-05","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Nielsen, Per Halkjær"],"keyword":["Anammox","Deammonification"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fast dna analyses for surveillance of microbial communities in full scale deammonification tanks potential for control and troubleshooting","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2023.119919","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["The project was funded by the Innovation Fund Denmark (OnlineDNA, grant 6155-00003B )."],"id":"S01-000.00-726487","identifier":[{"id":"37031530","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85151635389","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-ff3ddec4-cc29-4416-8a13-a36e94852477"],"keyword":["Anammox","Deammonification","Nanopore DNA sequencing","Process surveillance and control"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4BC3E7DA5D6E3AE42E2A336AA77BA0B1"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/688069931/1-s2.0-S004313542300355X-main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.watres.2023.119919","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, Department of Chemistry and Bioscience, Center for Microbial Communities"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"other companies","l2":"DI-PR","l2n":"Private Research","l3":"DI-PR-OTH","l3nl":"Other Companies","l3nn":"Other Companies","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Other Companies","namevar":["Krüger A/S"],"nora_code":"DI-PR-