 P.","orcid":"0000-0003-4893-012X","rank":26,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Ida","key":"moltkepau","last":"Moltke","locid":"9aab69f1-a07a-4a34-b844-9c7c61756cc5","name":"Moltke, Ida","orcid":"0000-0001-7052-8554","rank":27,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 4","first":"Torben","key":"hansenpau","last":"Hansen","locid":"6b6bfff1-8b14-468b-af77-e71b375018e0","name":"Hansen, Torben","orcid":"0000-0001-8748-3831","rank":28,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anders","key":"albrechtsenpau","last":"Albrechtsen","locid":"4b040c73-6b49-4e97-be60-5e34608e0615","name":"Albrechtsen, Anders","orcid":"0000-0001-7306-031X","rank":29,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34914943"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1053/j.gastro.2021.12.236","issn":["00165085","15280012"],"issn_org":["00165085"],"issue":"4","journal":"Gastroenterology","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Gastroenterology","volume":"162"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124939212"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Governmental Institutions","All Hospitals","All Miscellaneous","All Private Research","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-PR","DI-PR-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Novo Nordisk","Other Research","Private Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-85e14c2a-87d3-4d17-bb76-800a71a7620b","sdu-a0e191ac-a57d-4c2c-b7c9-ad9eb27deedd"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Loss of Sucrase-Isomaltase Function Increases Acetate Levels and Improves Metabolic Health in Greenlandic Cohorts","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-098.67-044070","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"The spotted wing drosophila (SWD), Drosophila suzukii, is a major invasive fruit pest. There is strong consensus that low temperature is among the main drivers of SWD population distribution, and the invasion success of SWD is also linked to its thermal plasticity. Most studies on ectotherm cold tolerance focus on exposure to a single stressful temperature but here we investigated how cold stress intensity affected survival duration across a broad range of low temperatures (−7 to +3 °C). The analysis of Lt50 at different stressful temperatures (Thermal Death Time curve - TDT) is based on the suggestion that cold injury accumulation rate increases exponentially with the intensity of thermal stress. In accordance with the hypothesis, Lt50 of SWD decreased exponentially with temperature. Further, comparison of TDT curves from flies acclimated to 15, 19 and 23 °C, respectively, showed an almost full compensation with acclimation such that the temperature required to induce mortality over a fixed time decreased almost 1 °C per °C lowering of acclimation temperature. Importantly, this change in cold tolerance with acclimation was uniform across the range of moderate to intense cold stress exposures examined. To understand if cold stress at moderate and intense exposures affects the same physiological systems we examined how physiological markers/symptoms of chill injury developed at different intensities of the cold stress. Specifically, hsp23 expression and extracellular [K+] were measured in flies exposed to different intensities of cold stress (−6, −2 and +2 °C) and at various time points corresponding to the same progression of injury (equivalent to 1/3, 2/3 or 3/3 of Lt50). The different cold stress intensities all triggered hsp23 expression following 2 h of recovery, but patterns of expression differed. At the most intense cold stress (−6 and −2 °C) a gradual increase with time was found. In contrast, at +2 °C an initial increase was followed by a dissipating expression. A gradual perturbation of ion balance (hyperkalemia) was also found at all three cold stress intensities examined, with only slight dissimilarities between treatment temperatures. Despite some differences between the three cold intensities examined, the results generally support the hypothesis that intense and moderate cold stress induces the same physiological perturbation. This suggests that cold stress experienced during natural fluctuating conditions is additive and the results also illustrate that the rate of injury accumulation increases dramatically (exponentially) with decreasing temperature (increasing stress).","author_count":6,"contributors":["Tarapacki, Pénélope","Jørgensen, Lisa Bjerregaard","Sørensen, Jesper Givskov","Andersen, Mads Kuhlmann","Colinet, Hervé","Overgaard, Johannes"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-205943"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Overgaard, Johannes"],"country":["Canada","France"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Carleton University","French National Centre for Scientific Research (CNRS)"],"keyword":["Chill tolerance","Heat shock proteins","Hyperkalemia","Invasive species","Ion balance","Thermal death time curves"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"acclimation duration and intensity of cold exposure determine the rate of cold stress accumulation and mortality in drosophila suzukii","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jinsphys.2021.104323","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-205943","identifier":[{"id":"34717940","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85118562694","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-86369125-7b49-4bbe-bfaf-528fd333ac5d"],"keyword":["Chill tolerance","Heat shock proteins","Hyperkalemia","Invasive species","Ion balance","Thermal death time curves"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-38AB82D5AE928A12B8C2551D86E5CC46"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/285213980/1_s2.0_S0022191021001335_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jinsphys.2021.104323","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University (AU)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ca","key":"carleton university","loc":["Canada","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Carleton University","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"fr","key":"french national centre for scientific research","loc":["France","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"French National Centre for Scientific Research (CNRS)","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Pénélope","key":"tarapackipau","last":"Tarapacki","name":"Tarapacki, Pénélope","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lisa Bjerregaard","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"51168cbb-ecf8-4fc1-bf24-eb80d125fba5","name":"Jørgensen, Lisa Bjerregaard","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jesper Givskov","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","locid":"ad745dfe-7c3b-4bbc-90df-4217c1b9ca92","name":"Sørensen, Jesper Givskov","orcid":"0000-0002-9149-3626","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mads Kuhlmann","key":"andersenpau","last":"Andersen","locid":"f6e2199f-b085-4b84-9c01-589a5dc95040","name":"Andersen, Mads Kuhlmann","orcid":"0000-0002-6215-8941","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Hervé","key":"colinetpau","last":"Colinet","name":"Colinet, Hervé","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Johannes","key":"overgaardpau","last":"Overgaard","locid":"71773655-48f6-45f8-9dec-fd660c342174","name":"Overgaard, Johannes","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34717940"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jinsphys.2021.104323","issn":["00221910","18791611"],"issn_org":["00221910"],"journal":"Journal of Insect Physiology","paperid":"104323","publisher":"Elsevier Ltd","status":"p","title":"Journal of Insect Physiology","volume":"135"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85118562694"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-86369125-7b49-4bbe-bfaf-528fd333ac5d"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Acclimation, duration and intensity of cold exposure determine the rate of cold stress accumulation and mortality in Drosophila suzukii","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-205943","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The genetic architecture of the small and isolated Greenlandic population is advantageous for identification of novel genetic variants associated with cardio-metabolic traits. We aimed to identify genetic loci associated with body mass index (BMI), to expand the knowledge of the genetic and biological mechanisms underlying obesity. Stage 1 BMI-association analyses were performed in 4,626 Greenlanders. Stage 2 replication and meta-analysis were performed in additional cohorts comprising 1,058 Yup'ik Alaska Native people, and 1,529 Greenlanders. Obesity-related traits were assessed in the stage 1 study population. We identified a common variant on chromosome 11, rs4936356, where the derived G-allele had a frequency of 24% in the stage 1 study population. The derived allele was genome-wide significantly associated with lower BMI (beta (SE), -0.14 SD (0.03), p = 3.2x10-8), corresponding to 0.64 kg/m2 lower BMI per G allele in the stage 1 study population. We observed a similar effect in the Yup'ik cohort (-0.09 SD, p = 0.038), and a non-significant effect in the same direction in the independent Greenlandic stage 2 cohort (-0.03 SD, p = 0.514). The association remained genome-wide significant in meta-analysis of the Arctic cohorts (-0.10 SD (0.02), p = 4.7x10-8). Moreover, the variant was associated with a leaner body type (weight, -1.68 (0.37) kg; waist circumference, -1.52 (0.33) cm; hip circumference, -0.85 (0.24) cm; lean mass, -0.84 (0.19) kg; fat mass and percent, -1.66 (0.33) kg and -1.39 (0.27) %; visceral adipose tissue, -0.30 (0.07) cm; subcutaneous adipose tissue, -0.16 (0.05) cm, all p<0.0002), lower insulin resistance (HOMA-IR, -0.12 (0.04), p = 0.00021), and favorable lipid levels (triglyceride, -0.05 (0.02) mmol/l, p = 0.025; HDL-cholesterol, 0.04 (0.01) mmol/l, p = 0.0015). In conclusion, we identified a novel variant, where the derived G-allele possibly associated with lower BMI in Arctic populations, and as a consequence also leaner body type, lower insulin resistance, and a favorable lipid profile.","author_count":24,"contributors":["Andersen, Mette K.","Jørsboe, Emil","Skotte, Line","Hanghøj, Kristian","Sandholt, Camilla Helene","Moltke, Ida","Grarup, Niels","Kern, Timo","Mahendran, Yuvaraj","Søborg, Bolette","Bjerregaard, Peter","Larsen, Christina V. L.","Dahl-Petersen, Inger K.","Tiwari, Hemant K.","Feenstra, Bjarke","Koch, Anders","Wiener, Howard W.","Hopkins, Scarlett E.","Pedersen, Oluf","Melbye, Mads","Boyer, Bert B.","Jørgensen, Marit E.","Albrechtsen, Anders","Hansen, Torben"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-043370"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Greenland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Grønlands Universitet","Oregon Health & Science University","PEPperPRINT GmbH","Stanford University","University of Alabama at Birmingham","University of Alaska"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the derived allele of a novel intergenic variant at chromosome 000000000011 associates with lower body mass index and a favorable metabolic phenotype in greenlanders","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1008544","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-043370","identifier":[{"id":"85079079642","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC7001991","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"31978080","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-70458cef-e154-4437-8804-f1e1a508e0a0","sdu-2a2f9b64-634c-48ce-ad53-9032984e118a"],"lang":"